  "text": "A model that wins in a notebook is still just a file. To be a product, it needs features at request time, an answer inside a latency budget, and labels coming back to say whether it was right. Designing that system is this round. Seventeen questions: eleven worked through, then six quickly.\n\nQuestion one. Batch versus online inference, and how do you choose? Batch scores everything on a schedule and stores the answers, like every user's recommendations, overnight. It's cheap and simple, but stale, and it can't use anything known only at request time. Online scores each request as it arrives, behind a low-latency service. It's fresh and can use context, like what's in the cart right now, but you own the latency budget, the scaling and the uptime. So choose by freshness and latency. A stored answer is only a lookup, so batch suits a heavy model on a tight budget, if yesterday's answer will do and every input is known in advance: you can't precompute a reply to a search nobody has typed yet. Many systems mix the two, with features cached in batch and light scoring online. Serving online brings this round's favorite bug.",
